From 956a21a985434a2d3619c8a4c9401301ca536924 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work8_fin_tc <adbtrr_work8_fin_tc@local>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 06:41:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] os/kernel/irq: Add ASSERT hitting test case for
 irq_unexpected_isr

irq_initialize() puts irq_unexpected_isr() in every vector slot, so it
runs when an interrupt fires for a vector nobody attached a handler to.
Nothing can service that interrupt and it would re-enter forever, so the
handler masks interrupts and calls PANIC() to bring the system down.

Add a negative test case which dispatches an interrupt with no handler
attached, so that the unconditional assertion of the module is executed.

PANIC() is built from ASSERT()/up_assert() and carries no CONFIG_DEBUG
dependency, so the test case works on a build with the debug features
turned off. The DEBUGASSERT() based checks of the module live in
irq_spinlock.c and irq_csection.c, which are built only for CONFIG_SMP
and fire only when CONFIG_DEBUG is enabled, so they are not used here.

 - os/drivers/os_api_test/kernel/test_assert_irq.c : kernel side helper
   reached through the TESTIOC_ASSERT_IRQ ioctl.
 - apps/examples/testcase/le_tc/kernel/tc_assert_irq.c : user side test
   case. A return from the ioctl is reported as a test case failure.

Controlled by CONFIG_TC_KERNEL_ASSERT_IRQ, default n, with no CONFIG_DEBUG
dependency. It crashes the board by design and must be enabled alone.
